A||N||11||TheFiero64||HELP! My New CD Radio||||5||
Z||000000||TheFiero64||03-14-2000||04:18 PM||||I have long been searching for a new radio because my clock will not shut off. I finally got a Pontiac UP3 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mar14^02.jpg">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mar14^01.jpg"> My problem is that the connector is different and I have no harness or diagram to hook it up. Dose anybody know where I can get a harness & diagram to hook it up. Please help me, thank you.||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||03-14-2000||04:40 PM||||I would try a junkyard for the connectors to plug into the radio. Make sure you get enough wiring to make splicing easy. You should be able to get the connector to plug into the Fiero harness at any place that does radio installs (maybe even WalMart).<P>Then *all* you have to do is figure out which wires to splice together. The Fiero Haynes manual or shop manual has the diagram for the Fiero end. For the CD end, maybe someone here has a diagram for the car it came from???<P>Good luck, David.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||03-14-2000||05:41 PM||||The Fiero64,<P>Do you need the harness as it attaches (male) to the head unit, or do you need the piggyback (female) connector to get the signals from your head units harness to the Fiero speaker wires?  If all you need is the piggyback harness, they are a common item.  They did change styles in 96 though.  You should be able to run to local store and purchase this for around $10 new.  If you need the male connector that plugs into the back of the radio, I would contact the seller.  These usually are the same within a manufacturer but are not required to be.  Also this piece is usually useless for the seller w/o a head unit since all aftermarket ones supply the harness and an OEM one might or might not swap out.  <P>Hope this helps,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000003||Monkeyman||03-14-2000||09:21 PM||||Try <A HREF="http://www.aatechnologies.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aatechnologies.com</A>  <BR>They sell factory radios, so maybe they have wiring harnesseseseses.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phaeton||03-14-2000||10:44 PM||||GM dealers have access to an adapter harness to connect old 12 pin GM radio's to the new 21 pin GM radio's.  It worked to put a cadillac seville CD player in an 88GT, can't remember the part # though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||zebrex||03-15-2000||02:41 AM||||I used to be a car stereo installer. My sugestion would be to go to your local car stereo shop if they sell Metra harnesses tell them you need a 70 series harness for a early model gm and a 71 series harness for a late model gm (I think the harness year switch is 87 or 88) your colors will match perfectly and you wont have to cut any wires but I think you will have to buy a motorolla to gm mini antenna adapter I dont remember the exact part numbers been awhile since Ive done that kind of work but this route will give you the cleanist results without any damage to the car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||03-15-2000||06:04 AM||||Try GM part # 16140051<P>let me know if it works out ok, I want to put that same unit in mine.<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||03-15-2000||08:54 AM||||I dont know about that radio, but all of my mid eighties radios have a wiring pin out stamped on the top of the radio.  I would think that GM stuck to the color coding as well.  <P>The harness end that plugs into your Fiero will be available at any Wal-Mart, or auto store that sells Car Stereo equipment.  The end that plugs in that radio youll need to get from a junkyard, or you might try checking with a place that installs car radios to see if they have a box of connectors that they have cut out of cars when they did installs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TheFiero64||03-15-2000||05:13 PM||||Thanks for all your replies. I am going to try my local Pontiac dealer tomorrow and see if he can help. I will post my result.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TheFiero64||03-16-2000||04:18 PM||||I got it! My friendly pontiac dealer had the part. Thanks RossT for getting me the part #. It was in stock and was a whole kit for the hookup. No Splicing   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Here is a pic of the kit.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mar16^02.jpg">  <P>If you are interested in the same radio here is the link <A HREF="http://www.aatechnologies.com/pmyradios/pontiac/16205302.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.aatechnologies.com/pmyradios/pontiac/16205302.asp</A> <P>Thanks again for everyone's help   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by TheFiero64 (edited 03-16-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000010||DRH||03-16-2000||04:38 PM||||Ain't this forum great!!!<P>If you don't mind my asking, how much did the harness cost?<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||TheFiero64||03-17-2000||12:14 PM||||It cost me $18.25 + tax. I never want to leave my car. wish I had a computer in there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
